=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"93\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"93\" y2=\"296\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"123.22222222222223\" y=\"209.33333333333331\" height=\"86.66666666666667\" width=\"30.22222222222222\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(148.25333333333333 315.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">African bush elephant</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"183.66666666666669\" y=\"256\" height=\"40\" width=\"30.22222222222222\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(208.6977777777778 315.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">eastern gray kangaroo</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"244.11111111111114\" y=\"169.33333333333331\" height=\"126.66666666666667\" width=\"30.22222222222222\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(269.1422222222223 315.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">wild boar</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"304.5555555555556\" y=\"115.99999999999997\" height=\"180.00000000000003\" width=\"30.22222222222222\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(329.58666666666676 315.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">white-tailed deer</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(229 24)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Top Four Species of Wild Land</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(229 48)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Mammals by Global Biomass</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(229 72)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" fill=\"none\" x=\"182.08534240722656\" y=\"477.2630615234375\" height=\"26.84\" width=\"93.82931518554688\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"189.08534240722656\" y=\"484.2630615234375\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(208.08534240722656 496.2630615234375)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">species</text></g></svg></figure><div class=\"sr-only\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for bar graph titled Top Four Species of Wild Land Mammals by Global Biomass\"><ul>\n<li>The Global biomass data for the 4 bars are as follows:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>African bush elephant: 1.3 million metric tons</li>\n<li>eastern gray kangaroo: 0.6 million metric tons</li>\n<li>wild boar: 1.9 million metric tons</li>\n<li>white-tailed deer: 2.7 million metric tons</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>Global biomass is the total mass of living material, such as animals and plants, on Earth. A team of scientists estimated the global biomass, by species, of various wild land mammals. The team found that the species with the highest global biomass is the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the sentence?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"305f65b9-df07-46b7-aab4-77e0105010cd","content":"<p>wild boar.</p>"},{"id":"a6781f16-e9a2-432a-ab08-09a98828b2cb","content":"<p>eastern gray kangaroo.</p>"},{"id":"283b9285-bb4f-4170-b3f0-3432af5a783f","content":"<p>African bush elephant.</p>"},{"id":"6a16b4d1-67df-4509-98b8-64ccc136ab49","content":"<p>white-tailed deer.</p>"}],"keys":["6a16b4d1-67df-4509-98b8-64ccc136ab49"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it accurately identifies the species with the highest global biomass, the white-tailed deer at approximately 2.7 million metric tons. The graph shows the global biomass for four wild land mammal species with the highest global biomass. The graph indicates that the African bush elephant&rsquo;s global biomass is about 1.3 million metric tons, the eastern gray kangaroo&rsquo;s is about 0.6 million metric tons, and the wild boar&rsquo;s is about 1.9 million metric tons. These values are all lower than the global biomass for the white-tailed deer&rsquo;s approximately 2.7 million metric tons. Thus, the white-tailed deer is the species with the highest global biomass.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because although the graph indicates that the wild boar has a relatively high global biomass of about 1.9 million metric tons, it is not the species with the highest value. The white-tailed deer is the species with the highest global biomass at about 2.7 million metric tons. Choice B is incorrect because the eastern gray kangaroo has the lowest global biomass value shown on the graph at about 0.6 million metric tons, not the highest global biomass. The white-tailed deer has the highest at about 2.7 million metric tons. Choice C is incorrect because although the African bush elephant has a substantial global biomass of about 1.3 million metric tons, it is not the species with the highest value according to the graph. The white-tailed deer has the highest global biomass at about 2.7 million metric tons.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669214,"updateDate":1743430669214}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669214, 1743430669214),
    ($SATQ$3091f805$SATQ$, $SATQ$8c905e66-b3bd-4010-973a-d51abc4a9009$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Ochre sea stars live in tidal pools along the shoreline of the Pacific Ocean. At night, they move to higher shore levels in search of prey. But scientists Corey Garza and Carlos Robles noticed that ochre sea stars stayed at lower levels at night after heavy rains. Garza and Robles hypothesized that a layer of fresh water formed by rainfall was a barrier to the sea stars. To test their hypothesis, the scientists did an experiment. They placed some sea stars in a climbable tank of seawater and other sea stars in a similar tank of seawater with a layer of fresh water on top. Then, the scientists watched the sea stars&rsquo; behavior at night.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding from the experiment, if true, would most directly support Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d437f8ba-d1d6-46c6-ac3c-aa1a302fbabb","content":"<p>None of the sea stars climbed to the tops of the tanks, but sea stars in the tank with only seawater moved around the bottom of the tank more than sea stars in the other tank did.</p>"},{"id":"f56672a0-3571-4699-9889-147409c9d455","content":"<p>Sea stars in the tank with only seawater climbed to the top of the tank, but sea stars in the other tank stopped climbing just below the layer of fresh water.</p>"},{"id":"7a0eedc0-23ac-4eac-940c-6278bb5970ba","content":"<p>Both groups of sea stars climbed to the tops of the tanks, but sea stars in the tank with only seawater climbed more slowly than sea stars in the other tank did.</p>"},{"id":"e45a218d-0f74-4b76-956e-a30b6c68e0e9","content":"<p>Sea stars in the tank with only seawater mostly stayed near the bottom of the tank, but sea stars in the other tank climbed into the layer of fresh water.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f56672a0-3571-4699-9889-147409c9d455"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would support Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis that a layer of fresh water forms a barrier to ochre sea stars. The text explains that the sea stars tend to move to higher shore levels at night in search of prey, but after a heavy rain, the sea stars stay at lower shore levels. Garza and Robles hypothesize that rainfall results in a layer of fresh water that the sea stars don&rsquo;t cross. To determine whether fresh water forms a barrier to sea stars, Garza and Robles observed how sea stars behaved in a tank of only seawater and in a tank of seawater with a layer of fresh water on top. If the sea stars climbed to the top of the tank with only seawater but stopped climbing just below the layer of fresh water in the other tank, that would suggest that fresh water does indeed serve as a barrier to the sea stars, thereby supporting Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that sea stars in the tank with only seawater moved around the bottom of the tank more than sea stars in the other tank did but that none of the stars in either tank climbed to the top would be irrelevant to Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis. Such a finding would reveal nothing about whether fresh water serves as a barrier to sea stars. Choice C is incorrect because finding that sea stars climbed to the top of both tanks would weaken, not support, Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis, since it would indicate that the layer of fresh water wasn&rsquo;t a barrier to the sea stars. Choice D is incorrect because finding that sea stars in the tank with only seawater mostly stayed near the bottom of the tank but sea stars in the other tank climbed into the layer of fresh water wouldn&rsquo;t support Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis. Instead, such a finding would suggest that the layer of fresh water wasn&rsquo;t a barrier to the sea stars, thereby weakening Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3091f805","external_id":"8c905e66-b3bd-4010-973a-d51abc4a9009","vaultid":null,"uId":"0bdb62f1-3139-4ab1-b7de-ab8bf3eff33d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Ochre sea stars live in tidal pools along the shoreline of the Pacific Ocean. At night, they move to higher shore levels in search of prey. But scientists Corey Garza and Carlos Robles noticed that ochre sea stars stayed at lower levels at night after heavy rains. Garza and Robles hypothesized that a layer of fresh water formed by rainfall was a barrier to the sea stars. To test their hypothesis, the scientists did an experiment. They placed some sea stars in a climbable tank of seawater and other sea stars in a similar tank of seawater with a layer of fresh water on top. Then, the scientists watched the sea stars&rsquo; behavior at night.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding from the experiment, if true, would most directly support Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d437f8ba-d1d6-46c6-ac3c-aa1a302fbabb","content":"<p>None of the sea stars climbed to the tops of the tanks, but sea stars in the tank with only seawater moved around the bottom of the tank more than sea stars in the other tank did.</p>"},{"id":"f56672a0-3571-4699-9889-147409c9d455","content":"<p>Sea stars in the tank with only seawater climbed to the top of the tank, but sea stars in the other tank stopped climbing just below the layer of fresh water.</p>"},{"id":"7a0eedc0-23ac-4eac-940c-6278bb5970ba","content":"<p>Both groups of sea stars climbed to the tops of the tanks, but sea stars in the tank with only seawater climbed more slowly than sea stars in the other tank did.</p>"},{"id":"e45a218d-0f74-4b76-956e-a30b6c68e0e9","content":"<p>Sea stars in the tank with only seawater mostly stayed near the bottom of the tank, but sea stars in the other tank climbed into the layer of fresh water.</p>"}],"keys":["f56672a0-3571-4699-9889-147409c9d455"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would support Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis that a layer of fresh water forms a barrier to ochre sea stars. The text explains that the sea stars tend to move to higher shore levels at night in search of prey, but after a heavy rain, the sea stars stay at lower shore levels. Garza and Robles hypothesize that rainfall results in a layer of fresh water that the sea stars don&rsquo;t cross. To determine whether fresh water forms a barrier to sea stars, Garza and Robles observed how sea stars behaved in a tank of only seawater and in a tank of seawater with a layer of fresh water on top. If the sea stars climbed to the top of the tank with only seawater but stopped climbing just below the layer of fresh water in the other tank, that would suggest that fresh water does indeed serve as a barrier to the sea stars, thereby supporting Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that sea stars in the tank with only seawater moved around the bottom of the tank more than sea stars in the other tank did but that none of the stars in either tank climbed to the top would be irrelevant to Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis. Such a finding would reveal nothing about whether fresh water serves as a barrier to sea stars. Choice C is incorrect because finding that sea stars climbed to the top of both tanks would weaken, not support, Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis, since it would indicate that the layer of fresh water wasn&rsquo;t a barrier to the sea stars. Choice D is incorrect because finding that sea stars in the tank with only seawater mostly stayed near the bottom of the tank but sea stars in the other tank climbed into the layer of fresh water wouldn&rsquo;t support Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis. Instead, such a finding would suggest that the layer of fresh water wasn&rsquo;t a barrier to the sea stars, thereby weakening Garza and Robles&rsquo;s hypothesis.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323044,"updateDate":1730147323044}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323044, 1730147323044),
